# Licensing

HERMES is split into three things that are licensed differently, because they
protect different things.

## 1. The code — MIT OR Apache-2.0

Everything in `src/`, `tools/`, and the build configuration is dual-licensed
under either of:

* Apache License, Version 2.0 — see [LICENSE-APACHE](LICENSE-APACHE)
* MIT License — see [LICENSE-MIT](LICENSE-MIT)

at your option. This is the standard Rust ecosystem arrangement: Apache-2.0
carries an explicit patent grant, MIT is maximally permissive and simple, and
you pick whichever fits your project.

Unless you state otherwise, any contribution you intentionally submit for
inclusion in this work shall be dual-licensed as above, with no additional
terms or conditions.

## 2. The icons — CC BY 4.0

Everything in `assets/` is licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International License (<https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>).

The icons are generated from geometric primitives by `tools/gen_icons.py` — no
traced artwork, no fonts, no third-party assets — so they are free of any
upstream encumbrance. See [assets/LICENSE](assets/LICENSE).

## 3. The name and the mark — reserved

**"HERMES", the HERMES word mark, and the HERMES icons as identifiers of this
project are NOT covered by the licenses above.**

This is not a formality, and it is not about branding. HERMES's entire security
model rests on a user trusting that the CLI on their machine is an honest,
empty shell — one that will refuse an unsigned update, refuse a path that
escapes its sandbox, and refuse folder access a plan did not declare. The name
and the icon are the only signals a user has that they are looking at a genuine
HERMES binary and a genuine `.origin` file.

So: fork the code freely, modify it freely, ship it freely. But if you
distribute a modified build, **give it your own name and your own icon.** A
patched binary that still calls itself HERMES and still shows the HERMES icon
is a phishing vector, not a fork.

You may of course use the name to refer to this project — "compatible with
HERMES", "built on HERMES", writing about it, packaging an unmodified build.
That is nominative use and it is fine.

## Summary

| What | License | You may |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `src/`, `tools/` | MIT OR Apache-2.0 | do essentially anything |
| `assets/` | CC BY 4.0 | reuse with attribution |
| The name "HERMES" and its marks | reserved | refer to the project, not rebadge a fork |
